/-!
# Ch. II §2 (second part): Lemma 8, Theorem 2 and its corollaries (pp. 193–197)

> Let A be a noetherian ring, and I an ideal in A, say I ≠ A. For each element f ∈ A, we define
> the symbol ν_I(f) as follows: ν_I(f) is the largest integer ν ≥ 0 such that f ∈ Iᵛ, if it
> exists, and ν_I(f) = ∞ if f ∈ Iᵛ for all positive integers ν. … If ν = ν_I(f) is a non-negative
> integer, then the class of f in Iᵛ/Iᵛ⁺¹ will be called the initial form of f in gr_I(A). …
> If J is an ideal in A, then we shall denote by gr_I(J, A) the graded ideal in gr_I(A)
> generated by the initial forms of elements of J. … We denote by grⁿ_I(J, A) the homogeneous part of
> degree n of the graded ideal gr_I(J, A).  (p. 189)

The definitions of p. 189 (`Ideal.ordElem` = ν_I, `initialForm`, `grIdeal` = gr_I(J, A)) and
Lemma 5 of p. 190 (`grMapQuot_surjective`, `ker_grMapQuot`) live in
`Hironaka/Compat/Graded.lean`.  Lemma 6, Lemma 7 (pp. 190–193), the condition (iii) of Theorem 2
as a named predicate `HasInitialFormSystem` and the auxiliary statements for Lemma 8 are in
`Hironaka/Ch2/GradedIdealBase.lean`; Lemma 8 itself (pp. 193–194) is proved by the printed diagram
in `Hironaka/Ch2/Lemma8Diagram.lean`.  This file contains the rest of §2 in the paper's order:
the statement of Lemma 8 for a system `y` with `Q = P + (y)`, Theorem 2 and its two corollaries
(pp. 193–197).

## Standing setup (pp. 190–195)

`R` is a regular local ring, `J` an ideal in `R`, `P` and `Q` prime ideals with `Q ⊇ P ⊇ J`
such that `R/P` and `R/Q` are both regular.  Following pp. 193–195, `O = R/J`, `P̄ = P/J`,
`Q̄ = Q/J`, `Ō = O/P̄ (= R/P)` and `Q̄' = Q̄/P̄ (= Q/P)`, an ideal of `Ō`.  In Lean:
* `O` is `R ⧸ J`; `P̄` is `P.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J)`; `Q̄` is `Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J)`;
* `Ō` is `O ⧸ P̄`; `Q̄'` is `Q̄.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P̄)`.

The graded objects are those of `Hironaka/Compat/Graded.lean` and `Hironaka/Ch2/Flatness.lean`:
* `gr_{P̄}(O)` is `AssocGraded P̄`, an `Ō`-algebra (`AssocGraded.instAlgebraQuotient`);
* `gr_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is the associated graded module of the `Ō`-module `gr_{P̄}(O)` with
  respect to the ideal `Q̄ gr_{P̄}(O) = Q̄' gr_{P̄}(O)`; its homogeneous part of degree `m`,
  `grᵐ_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) = Q̄'ᵐ gr_{P̄}(O)/Q̄'ᵐ⁺¹ gr_{P̄}(O)`, is
  `GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m`, a module over `Ō/Q̄'`; in particular
  `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is `GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0`;
* `gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) = gr_{Q̄'}(Ō)` has homogeneous parts `GrPiece Q̄' m`;
* the canonical homomorphism `φ` of Theorem 2 (iv),
  `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → gr_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))`, is the canonical `φ` of
  Lemma 2 of §1 (p. 182) for the `Ō`-module `M = gr_{P̄}(O)` and the ideal `I = Q̄'`, i.e. the
  direct sum over `m` of the maps `grModMap Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m`; as in §1, "`φ` is an
  isomorphism" is transcribed as `∀ m, Function.Bijective (grModMap Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m)`
  (see `def:II.1-canonical-phi`);
* "`gr_{P̄}(O)` is flat over `O/P̄`" (`O` a noetherian local ring) is `IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal P̄`
  (Definition 1 of Ch. 0, p. 136, and Lemma 1 of §1), and "`gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is flat over
  `O/Q̄`" is `Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0)`: the paper's `O/Q̄`
  is identified with `Ō/Q̄'` through the canonical isomorphism `O/Q̄ ≅ (O/P̄)/(Q̄/P̄)`
  (`DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotOfLE`), and `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is a module over `O/Q̄`
  through it.

## The homomorphism `ψ'` and Lemma 8 (pp. 193–194)

On p. 193 the paper chooses "a system of elements `(y₁, …, y_s)` in `R` such that the residue
classes `(ȳ₁, …, ȳ_s)` of them in `Ō` form a minimal base of `Q̄'` (`s = dim Ō_{Q̄'}`)" and
uses this choice to build the homomorphism of graded `O/Q̄`-algebras
  `ψ' : gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → gr_{Q̄}(O)`.
`ψ'` depends on the choice of `(y₁, …, y_s)` and is not canonical; it is not a definition here,
but `psiDeg` of `Hironaka/Ch2/Flatness.lean` (the map `ψ` of Proposition 1 of §1, for the local
ring `O`, `P̄`, `Q̄` and the classes of the `y_j`, one total degree at a time) takes the system as
an argument.  In the formalization:
* **Lemma 8 (p. 194)** is `bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem`, proved by the printed
  diagram in `Hironaka/Ch2/Lemma8Diagram.lean` (`Lemma8.bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem`):
  "`ψ'` is an isomorphism if and only if there
  exists a system `(f₁, …, f_m)` of elements of `J` such that (1) `ν_Q(f_j) = ν_P(f_j)` and
  (2) `gr_Q(J, R) = (φ₁, …, φ_m) gr_Q(R)`", the right-hand side being the predicate
  `HasInitialFormSystem J P Q` below.  This is the version for a system `y` with `Q = P + (y)` and
  the classes `ȳ_j` quasi-regular in `R/P`; the paper's form, for `y` whose classes form a minimal
  base of `Q/P`, is `bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem_of_span_eq_map` in
  `Hironaka/Ch2/AscendingChain.lean`, where it is proved that a minimal base has these two
  properties (deviation from the paper (gap filled)).
  "`ψ'` is an isomorphism" means bijective in each total degree.
* **Condition (ii) of Theorem 2 and of Corollary 1** ("`gr_{Q̄}(O)` is flat over `O/Q̄` and `ψ'`
  is isomorphic") is restored in `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_psiDeg` and
  `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_psiDeg_of_eq_maximalIdeal` (and, in the paper's form for
  minimal bases, in `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_psiDeg_of_span_eq_map` and
  `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_psiDeg_of_eq_maximalIdeal_of_span_eq_map`), with the paper's
  proof: (i) ⟹ (ii)
  by Proposition 1 of §1, (ii) ⟺ (iii) by Lemma 8, (iv) ⟹ (i) by the corollary to Lemma 2 of
  §1, (ii) + (iii) ⟹ (iv) as below.  The statements `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae` and
  `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_of_eq_maximalIdeal` (conditions (i), (iii), (iv) only) are
  kept, as the rest of the project uses them; their proofs are the paper's, with (ii) as the
  intermediate step for a system `y` as on pp. 183 and 193 (`exists_adapted_lift`): (i) ⟹ (ii) is
  Proposition 1 of §1, (ii) ⟺ (iii) is Lemma 8, (ii) + (iii) ⟹ (iv) is
  `flat_and_bijective_grModMap_of_bijective_psiDeg`, and (iv) ⟹ (i) is the corollary to Lemma 2
  of §1; see the docstring of `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae`.
-/

@[expose] public section

open AlgebraicGeometry

namespace Hironaka

universe u

/-! ### Lemma 8 (pp. 193–194) -/

section Lemma8Iff

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] (J P Q : Ideal R) {s : ℕ} (y : Fin s → R)

/-- Lemma 8, p. 194: "Notation and assumptions being as above,
`ψ' : gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → gr_{Q̄}(O)` is an isomorphism of graded
`O/Q̄ (= Ō/Q̄')`-algebras if and only if there exists a system of elements `(f₁, …, f_m)` of `J`
such that (1) `ν_Q(f_j) = ν_P(f_j)` for `1 ≤ j ≤ m`, and (2) if `φ_j` denotes the initial form of
`f_j` in `gr_Q(R)`, then `gr_Q(J, R) = (φ₁, …, φ_m) gr_Q(R)`."  The right-hand side is
`HasInitialFormSystem J P Q`, the left-hand side is bijectivity of `psiDeg` (the map `ψ'` of
p. 193, which is `ψ` of Proposition 1 of §1 for `O`, `P̄`, `Q̄` and the classes of the `y_j`; see
`psiDeg` in `Hironaka/Ch2/Flatness.lean`).

Intermediate version (the paper's form is `bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem_of_span_eq_map`
in `AscendingChain.lean`): `ψ'` depends on the choice of the system `(y₁, …, y_s)` of p. 193 and the
paper says "Notation and assumptions being as above"; here the lemma is stated for every system `y`
whose classes in `R/P` form a minimal base of `Q/P` in the sense of the hypotheses `hQY`
(`Q = P + (y)`) and `hqr` (the `ȳ_j` are quasi-regular in `R/P`, i.e. part of a regular system of
parameters of `R/P`; the paper takes this for granted, cf. the deviation "gap filled" of
`exists_mul_mem_grIdeal_of_isLambdaImage_of_span_eq_map`); the corresponding hypotheses for
`Ō = O/P̄` are derived (`isQuasiRegular_map_quotient`, `span_map_mk_eq_map_quotient`).  "`ψ'` is an
isomorphism" means that `ψ'` is bijective in each total degree.

The proof is the printed one (the diagram of p. 194, right exactness of `⊗`, Lemma 5, the map
`γ : gr_P(R) → gr_Q(R)` induced by `Pᵛ ⊆ Qᵛ`), carried out in `Hironaka/Ch2/Lemma8Diagram.lean`:
this is `Lemma8.bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem`. -/
@[blueprint "lem:II.2-8-quasi-regular"
  (statement := /-- Notation and assumptions being as above (\cref{thm:II.2-2}),
    $\psi'\colon\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}
    \operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)\to\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$ is an isomorphism
    of graded $O/\bar Q$ ($=\bar O/\bar Q'$)-algebras if and only if there exists a system of
    elements $(f_1,\dots,f_m)$ of $J$ such that (1) $\nu_Q(f_j)=\nu_P(f_j)$ for
    $1\le j\le m$, and (2) if $\varphi_j$ denotes the initial form of $f_j$ in
    $\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$, then $\operatorname{gr}_Q(J,R)=(\varphi_1,\dots,\varphi_m)
    \operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$ (\cref{def:II.2-initial-form-system}). (Lemma 8, p.~194.)

    \textbf{Formalization note (intermediate version).} The paper's form is
    \cref{lem:II.2-8}. $\psi'$ depends on the system
    $(y_1,\dots,y_s)$ of p.~193 whose classes in $R/P$ form a minimal base of $Q/P$; the lemma is
    stated for every such system, encoded as $Q=P+(y)$ with the $\bar y_j$ quasi-regular in
    $R/P$ (part of a regular system of parameters of $R/P$, which the paper takes for granted),
    and $\psi'$ is $\psi$ of \cref{prop:II.1-1-psi} for $O$, $\bar P$, $\bar Q$ and the classes
    of the $y_j$, ``isomorphism'' meaning bijective in each total degree. -/)
  (proof := /-- Consider the commutative diagram of $O/\bar Q$-modules (p.~194) with the
    rows $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_P(J,R))\otimes\operatorname{gr}_{\bar
    Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)\xrightarrow{\psi''}\operatorname{gr}_Q(J,R)$,
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_P(R))\otimes\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar
    P}(O/\bar P)\xrightarrow{\psi}\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$ and
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))\otimes\operatorname{gr}_{\bar
    Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)\xrightarrow{\psi'}\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$. The two vertical
    sequences are exact by \cref{lem:II.2-5} and by the right exactness of the tensor product.
    Moreover $\psi$ is isomorphic and $\psi'$ is surjective. Therefore $\psi'$ is isomorphic if and
    only if $\psi''$ is surjective. Let $\gamma\colon\operatorname{gr}_P(R)\to\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$
    be induced by the inclusions $P^\nu\subseteq Q^\nu$. The image of $\psi''$ is the graded ideal of
    $\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$ generated by the image of $\operatorname{gr}_P(J,R)$ under $\gamma$, so
    $\psi'$ is isomorphic if and only if the image of $\operatorname{gr}_P(J,R)$ under $\gamma$
    generates $\operatorname{gr}_Q(J,R)$. For $f\in J$ put $\nu=\nu_P(f)$, $\mu=\nu_Q(f)$, so
    $\nu\le\mu$. If $\nu<\mu$, the initial form $\Phi$ of $f$ in $\operatorname{gr}_P(R)$ is such that
    $\deg\Phi=\nu$ and $\Phi\in Q^{\mu-\nu}\operatorname{gr}_P(R)$; the image of $\Phi$ in
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_P(R))$ is then zero, hence $\gamma(\Phi)=0$. If
    $\nu=\mu$, then $\gamma(\Phi)$ is the initial form of $f$ in $\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$
    ($\gamma$ is induced by the inclusion of $P^\nu$ into $Q^\nu$). Thus the lemma is
    verified. This is the proof of \cref{lem:II.2-8}, for the system $y$ of the statement. -/)
  (latexEnv := "lemma")]
theorem bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem [IsRegularLocalRing R] [P.IsPrime] [Q.IsPrime]
    [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P)] [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ Q)] (hJP : J ≤ P) (hPQ : P ≤ Q)
    (hQY : Q = P ⊔ Ideal.span (Set.range y))
    (hqr : IsQuasiRegular fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk P (y j)) :
    (∀ n, Function.Bijective (psiDeg (P.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J)) (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J))
      (Ideal.map_mono hPQ) (fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk J (y j))
      (isQuasiRegular_map_quotient J P y hJP hqr) (span_map_mk_eq_map_quotient J P Q y hQY) n)) ↔
        HasInitialFormSystem J P Q :=
  Lemma8.bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem J P Q y hJP hPQ hQY hqr

end Lemma8Iff

/-! ### Flatness of `gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O))` from the isomorphism `ψ'` (p. 195) -/

section PsiFlat

variable {O : Type u} [CommRing O] (P m : Ideal O) (hPm : P ≤ m) {s : ℕ} (y : Fin s → O)
  (hqr : IsQuasiRegular fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk P (y j))
  (hz : Ideal.span (Set.range fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk P (y j)) =
    m.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P))

include hPm hqr hz in
/-- `gr^0_{m̄}(Ō) = Ō/m̄` is free of rank one over `O/m`, the degree-`0` part of the basis of
monomials. -/
theorem nonempty_linearEquiv_grPiece_map_zero :
    Nonempty (GrPiece (m.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) 0 ≃ₗ[O ⧸ m] (O ⧸ m)) := by
  classical
  have : Unique ↥(degreeFinset s 0) :=
    ⟨⟨⟨0, mem_degreeFinset.mpr (by simp)⟩⟩, fun a => Subtype.ext (by
      have := mem_degreeFinset.mp a.2
      exact (Finsupp.degree_eq_zero_iff _).mp this) ⟩
  exact ⟨(psiBasis P m hPm y hqr hz 0).equivFun.trans (LinearEquiv.funUnique _ (O ⧸ m) (O ⧸ m))⟩

include hPm hqr hz in
/-- **The direct summands of `grⁿ_m(O)` are free** (p. 195): "First of all, the isomorphism `ψ'`
of (ii) induces isomorphisms of `O/Q̄`-modules
`⊕_{p+q=n} gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)` for all non-negative
integers `n`, where `⊕` signifies the direct sum.  By the assumption that `gr_{Q̄}(O)` is flat over
`O/Q̄`, `grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)` must be a free `O/Q̄`-module, and hence the direct summands
`gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` are such also (see Lemma 1 of §1)."

Here `m` is the paper's `Q̄` and `O` its `O`, `P` its `P̄`.  "`grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)` is free" is part of the
definition of `IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal` (the paper's "flat", equivalent by Lemma 1, p. 136); the
summand `E_{pq}` is the image of the inclusion `lof` of the direct sum `⊕_{p'+q'=n} E_{p'q'}`,
which `ψ'` identifies with the free module `grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)`, and is a retract of it, hence projective
(`Module.Projective.of_split`); it is of finite type over the local ring `O/Q̄`, hence free by
Lemma 1 of §1 (`free_projective_flat_tfae`). -/
@[blueprint "lem:II.2-2-psi-summand-free"
  (statement := /-- In the notation of \cref{thm:II.2-2}, let $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$ be
    flat over $O/\bar Q$ and let $\psi'$ be bijective in total degree $n$. Then each direct summand
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}
    \operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)$, $p+q=n$, of the source of $\psi'$ in degree $n$ is
    a free $O/\bar Q$-module. (Theorem 2, p.~195.) -/)
  (proof := /-- The isomorphism $\psi'$ identifies $\bigoplus_{p+q=n}\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}
    (\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar P}(O))\otimes\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)$ with
    $\operatorname{gr}^n_{\bar Q}(O)$, which is a free $O/\bar Q$-module; so each summand is a
    projective $O/\bar Q$-module of finite type, hence free by \cref{lem:II.1-1}. -/)
  (latexEnv := "lemma")]
theorem free_psiSummand_of_bijective_psiDeg [IsLocalRing O] [IsNoetherianRing O] (hm1 : m ≠ ⊤)
    (hm : IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal m) (n : ℕ)
    (hbij : Function.Bijective (psiDeg P m hPm y hqr hz n))
    (pq : {pq : ℕ × ℕ // pq.1 + pq.2 = n}) :
    Module.Free (O ⧸ m) (PsiSummand P m n pq) := by
  classical
  have : Nontrivial (O ⧸ m) := Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mpr hm1
  have : IsLocalRing (O ⧸ m) :=
    IsLocalRing.of_surjective' (Ideal.Quotient.mk m) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  -- `E_{pq}` is of finite type over the local ring `O/m`
  have h1 : Module.Finite (O ⧸ m) (GrModPiece m (GrPiece P pq.1.1) 0) := inferInstance
  have h2 : Module.Finite (O ⧸ m) (GrPiece (m.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) pq.1.2) :=
    Module.Finite.of_basis (psiBasis P m hPm y hqr hz pq.1.2)
  have hfin : Module.Finite (O ⧸ m) (PsiSummand P m n pq) :=
    Module.Finite.tensorProduct (O ⧸ m) _ _
  -- `grⁿ_m(O)` is free, hence so is `⨁_{p+q=n} E_{pq}` (by `ψ'`), and `E_{pq}` is a retract of it
  have hn : Module.Free (O ⧸ m) (GrPiece m n) := hm n
  have hfree : Module.Free (O ⧸ m)
      (DirectSum {pq : ℕ × ℕ // pq.1 + pq.2 = n} (PsiSummand P m n)) :=
    Module.Free.of_equiv (LinearEquiv.ofBijective (psiDeg P m hPm y hqr hz n) hbij).symm
  have hproj : Module.Projective (O ⧸ m) (PsiSummand P m n pq) :=
    Module.Projective.of_split (DirectSum.lof (O ⧸ m) _ (PsiSummand P m n) pq)
      (DirectSum.component (O ⧸ m) _ (PsiSummand P m n) pq)
      (LinearMap.ext fun v => DirectSum.component.lof_self (O ⧸ m) pq v)
  -- Lemma 1 of §1: a projective module of finite type over a local ring is free
  have hlemma1 : Module.Projective (O ⧸ m) (PsiSummand P m n pq) ↔
      Module.Free (O ⧸ m) (PsiSummand P m n pq) :=
    (free_projective_flat_tfae (O ⧸ m) (PsiSummand P m n pq)).out 2 1
  exact hlemma1.mp hproj

include hPm hqr hz in
/-- **`gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O))` is free over `O/Q̄`** (p. 195): "Note that `gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` are free
`(O/Q̄)`-modules.  Therefore `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is flat over `O/Q̄`", for the piece of
`gr_{P̄}(O) = ⊕ₚ grᵖ_{P̄}(O)` of degree `p`: it is the summand `E_{p,0}` (`q = 0`) of the free
module `grᵖ_{Q̄}(O)` (`free_psiSummand_of_bijective_psiDeg`), because
`gr⁰_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) = O/Q̄` is free of rank one. -/
theorem free_grModPiece_zero_grPiece_of_bijective_psiDeg [IsLocalRing O] [IsNoetherianRing O]
    (hm1 : m ≠ ⊤) (hm : IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal m) (p : ℕ)
    (hbij : Function.Bijective (psiDeg P m hPm y hqr hz p)) :
    Module.Free (O ⧸ m) (GrModPiece m (GrPiece P p) 0) := by
  have h := free_psiSummand_of_bijective_psiDeg P m hPm y hqr hz hm1 hm p hbij ⟨(p, 0), rfl⟩
  obtain ⟨e0⟩ := nonempty_linearEquiv_grPiece_map_zero P m hPm y hqr hz
  let eT : PsiSummand P m p ⟨(p, 0), rfl⟩ ≃ₗ[O ⧸ m] GrModPiece m (GrPiece P p) 0 :=
    (TensorProduct.congr (LinearEquiv.refl (O ⧸ m) (GrModPiece m (GrPiece P p) 0)) e0).trans
      (TensorProduct.rid (O ⧸ m) (GrModPiece m (GrPiece P p) 0))
  exact Module.Free.of_equiv eT

end PsiFlat

/-! ### Transport of freeness along `(Ō/Q̄') ≅ O/Q̄` -/

section TransportFree

/-- Freeness is transported along a ring isomorphism `K ≃+* B` and an additive equivalence
`N₁ ≃+ N₂` which is semilinear with respect to it. -/
theorem free_of_addEquiv_of_ringEquiv {K B N₁ N₂ : Type*} [CommRing K] [CommRing B]
    [AddCommGroup N₁] [Module K N₁] [AddCommGroup N₂] [Module B N₂] (e : K ≃+* B)
    (f : N₁ ≃+ N₂) (hf : ∀ (k : K) (x : N₁), f (k • x) = e k • f x) [Module.Free B N₂] :
    Module.Free K N₁ := by
  classical
  let b := Module.Free.chooseBasis B N₂
  let F₀ : N₁ →+ (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex B N₂ →₀ K) :=
    { toFun := fun x => Finsupp.mapRange e.symm (map_zero _) (b.repr (f x))
      map_zero' := by simp
      map_add' := fun x y => by
        ext i
        simp }
  have hF : ∀ (k : K) (x : N₁), F₀ (k • x) = k • F₀ x := by
    intro k x
    ext i
    show e.symm (b.repr (f (k • x)) i) = k * e.symm (b.repr (f x) i)
    rw [hf, map_smul, Finsupp.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul, map_mul, e.symm_apply_apply]
  let F : N₁ →ₗ[K] (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex B N₂ →₀ K) :=
    { F₀ with map_smul' := fun k x => by simpa using hF k x }
  have hbij : Function.Bijective F := by
    constructor
    · intro x y hxy
      have : Finsupp.mapRange e.symm (map_zero _) (b.repr (f x)) =
          Finsupp.mapRange e.symm (map_zero _) (b.repr (f y)) := hxy
      have h2 := Finsupp.mapRange_injective (α := Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex B N₂)
        e.symm (map_zero _) e.symm.injective this
      exact f.injective (b.repr.injective h2)
    · intro r
      refine ⟨f.symm (b.repr.symm (Finsupp.mapRange e (map_zero _) r)), ?_⟩
      show Finsupp.mapRange e.symm (map_zero _) (b.repr (f (f.symm (b.repr.symm
        (Finsupp.mapRange e (map_zero _) r))))) = r
      ext i
      simp
  exact Module.Free.of_equiv (LinearEquiv.ofBijective F hbij).symm

section ModuleQuotients

variable {O : Type u} [CommRing O] (P Q : Ideal O) (hPQ : P ≤ Q) (G : Type u) [AddCommGroup G]
  [Module O G] [Module (O ⧸ P) G] [IsScalarTower O (O ⧸ P) G]

/-- `Q̄'ᵏ G = Qᵏ G` as subsets of an `O`-module `G` which is an `O/P`-module compatibly, `Q̄' = Q/P`
(`ideal of `O/P`). -/
theorem smul_top_map_eq (k : ℕ) :
    ((Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) ^ k • (⊤ : Submodule (O ⧸ P) G)).restrictScalars O =
      Q ^ k • (⊤ : Submodule O G) := by
  have hpow : (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) ^ k = (Q ^ k).map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P) :=
    (Ideal.map_pow _ _ _).symm
  rw [hpow]
  ext x
  simp only [Submodule.restrictScalars_mem]
  constructor
  · intro hx
    refine Submodule.smul_induction_on hx (fun a ha g _ => ?_) (fun _ _ h h' => add_mem h h')
    obtain ⟨o, ho, rfl⟩ := (Ideal.mem_map_iff_of_surjective _ Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective).mp ha
    have : Ideal.Quotient.mk P o • g = o • g := algebraMap_smul (O ⧸ P) o g
    rw [this]
    exact Submodule.smul_mem_smul ho Submodule.mem_top
  · intro hx
    refine Submodule.smul_induction_on hx (fun a ha g _ => ?_) (fun _ _ h h' => add_mem h h')
    have : a • g = Ideal.Quotient.mk P a • g := (algebraMap_smul (O ⧸ P) a g).symm
    rw [this]
    exact Submodule.smul_mem_smul (Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ ha) Submodule.mem_top

theorem exists_addEquiv_grModPiece_zero_map :
    ∃ f : GrModPiece (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) G 0 ≃+ GrModPiece Q G 0,
      ∀ (k : ((O ⧸ P) ⧸ Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)))
        (x : GrModPiece (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) G 0),
        f (k • x) = (DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotOfLE hPQ k) • f x := by
  let p₁ : Submodule (O ⧸ P) G := (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) • ⊤
  let p₂ : Submodule O G := Q • ⊤
  have hp : p₁.restrictScalars O = p₂ := by
    have := smul_top_map_eq P Q G 1
    simpa [pow_one] using this
  let eA : (G ⧸ p₁) ≃ₗ[O ⧸ P] GrModPiece (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) G 0 :=
    quotEquivGrModPieceZero _ G
  let eB : (G ⧸ p₂) ≃ₗ[O] GrModPiece Q G 0 := quotEquivGrModPieceZero _ G
  let mid : (G ⧸ p₁) ≃ₗ[O] (G ⧸ p₂) :=
    (Submodule.Quotient.restrictScalarsEquiv O p₁).symm.trans (Submodule.quotEquivOfEq _ _ hp)
  refine ⟨eA.symm.toAddEquiv.trans (mid.toAddEquiv.trans eB.toAddEquiv), ?_⟩
  intro k x
  obtain ⟨c, rfl⟩ := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective k
  obtain ⟨o, rfl⟩ := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective c
  have h1 : (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk P o)) • x =
      (Ideal.Quotient.mk P o) • x :=
    algebraMap_smul ((O ⧸ P) ⧸ Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk P o) x
  have h2 : DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotOfLE hPQ
      (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Q.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk P o)) =
        Ideal.Quotient.mk Q o := rfl
  have h3 : (Ideal.Quotient.mk P o) • eA.symm x = o • eA.symm x := algebraMap_smul (O ⧸ P) o _
  have h4 : (Ideal.Quotient.mk Q o) • (eB (mid (eA.symm x))) = o • (eB (mid (eA.symm x))) :=
    algebraMap_smul (O ⧸ Q) o _
  rw [h2, h1]
  show eB (mid (eA.symm ((Ideal.Quotient.mk P o) • x))) = (Ideal.Quotient.mk Q o) •
    (eB (mid (eA.symm x)))
  rw [map_smul, h3, map_smul, map_smul, h4]

end ModuleQuotients

end TransportFree

/-! ### Theorem 2 and Corollary 1 (pp. 195–197) -/

section Theorem2

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R] (J P Q : Ideal R) [P.IsPrime]
  [Q.IsPrime] [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P)] [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ Q)]

-- The notation of pp. 193–195 (see the module docstring): `O = R/J`, `P̄ = P/J`, `Q̄ = Q/J`,
-- `Ō = O/P̄ (= R/P)` and `Q̄' = Q̄/P̄ (= Q/P)`, an ideal of `Ō`.
local notation "𝒪" => R ⧸ J
local notation "P̄" => Ideal.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J) P
local notation "Q̄" => Ideal.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J) Q
local notation "Ō" => 𝒪 ⧸ P̄
local notation "Q̄'" => Ideal.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk P̄) Q̄

/-- Generators adapted to `P ⊆ Q` (p. 191, footnote 41: "cf. footnote 39"; see
`exists_adapted_quasiRegular`), in the form used below: `Q = P + Y` with independent bidegree
components. -/
theorem exists_complement_indep (hPQ : P ≤ Q) :
    ∃ Y : Ideal R, Q = P ⊔ Y ∧ ∀ (m : ℕ) (s : Finset ℕ), (∀ a ∈ s, a ≤ m) → ∀ u : ℕ → R,
      (∀ a ∈ s, u a ∈ P ^ a * Y ^ (m - a)) → ∑ a ∈ s, u a ∈ Q ^ (m + 1) →
      ∀ a ∈ s, u a ∈ Q * (P ^ a * Y ^ (m - a)) := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨N, x, Zs, hqr, hxQ, hxP⟩ :=
    exists_adapted_quasiRegular P Q hPQ inferInstance inferInstance
  refine ⟨Ideal.span (x '' ↑Zsᶜ), ?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [← hxQ, ← hxP, ← Ideal.span_union, ← Set.image_union, Finset.coe_compl,
      Set.union_compl_self, Set.image_univ]
  · intro m s hs u hu hsum
    rw [← hxP, ← hxQ] at *
    exact mem_mul_of_sum_mem_pow x hqr Zs m s hs u hu hsum

/-- The homogeneous part of degree `n` of `gr_I(A)` consists of the classes `[a Xⁿ]`,
`a ∈ Iⁿ`. -/
private theorem assocGraded_mem_homogeneous_iff {A : Type u} [CommRing A] (I : Ideal A) (n : ℕ)
    (φ : AssocGraded I) :
    φ ∈ AssocGraded.homogeneous I n ↔
      ∃ (a : A) (ha : a ∈ I ^ n), AssocGraded.mk I n a ha = φ := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨y, rfl⟩
    induction y using Submodule.Quotient.induction_on with
    | H x => exact ⟨x.1, x.2, rfl⟩
  · rintro ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩
    exact AssocGraded.mk_mem_homogeneous I ha

/-- The homogeneous parts of `gr_I(A)` form a graded monoid: `1 = [1 X⁰]` has degree `0` and
`[a Xᵐ] · [b Xⁿ] = [ab Xᵐ⁺ⁿ]`. -/
private theorem assocGraded_gradedMonoid {A : Type u} [CommRing A] (I : Ideal A) :
    SetLike.GradedMonoid (AssocGraded.homogeneous I) where
  one_mem := by
    rw [assocGraded_mem_homogeneous_iff]
    refine ⟨1, by simp, ?_⟩
    rw [← AssocGraded.ofRees_monomial, ← map_one (AssocGraded.ofRees I)]
    congr 1
  mul_mem := by
    intro i j a b ha hb
    rw [assocGraded_mem_homogeneous_iff] at ha hb ⊢
    obtain ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩ := ha
    obtain ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩ := hb
    exact ⟨_, _, (AssocGraded.mk_mul_mk I ha hb).symm⟩

/-- `gr_I(A)` is the internal direct sum of its homogeneous parts (`toGr_bijective`). -/
private theorem assocGraded_isInternal {A : Type u} [CommRing A] (I : Ideal A) :
    DirectSum.IsInternal (AssocGraded.homogeneous I) := by
  let e : ∀ n, GrPiece I n ≃ₗ[A ⧸ I] AssocGraded.homogeneous I n := fun n =>
    LinearEquiv.ofInjective _ (AssocGraded.ofGrPiece_injective I n)
  have key : DirectSum.coeLinearMap (AssocGraded.homogeneous I) ∘ₗ
      DirectSum.lmap (fun n => (e n).toLinearMap) = toGr I := by
    refine DirectSum.linearMap_ext _ fun n => LinearMap.ext fun y => ?_
    simp only [LinearMap.comp_apply, DirectSum.lmap_lof, DirectSum.coeLinearMap_lof, toGr,
      DirectSum.toModule_lof]
    rfl
  have hlmap : Function.Bijective (DirectSum.lmap (fun n => (e n).toLinearMap)) :=
    ⟨(DirectSum.lmap_injective _).mpr fun n => (e n).injective,
      (DirectSum.lmap_surjective _).mpr fun n => (e n).surjective⟩
  have hcomp : Function.Bijective (DirectSum.coeLinearMap (AssocGraded.homogeneous I) ∘
      DirectSum.lmap (fun n => (e n).toLinearMap)) := by
    rw [← LinearMap.coe_comp, key]
    exact toGr_bijective I
  exact (Function.Bijective.of_comp_iff _ hlmap).mp hcomp

/-- For noetherian `O`, `gr_P(O)` is an `O/P`-algebra of finite type generated by its
homogeneous elements of degree `1`. -/
private theorem assocGraded_finiteType_and_adjoin {O : Type u} [CommRing O] [IsNoetherianRing O]
    (P : Ideal O) :
    Algebra.FiniteType (O ⧸ P) (AssocGraded P) ∧
      Algebra.adjoin (O ⧸ P)
        (⋃ n : ℕ, (AssocGraded.homogeneous P (n + 1) : Set (AssocGraded P))) = ⊤ := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := (IsNoetherian.noetherian P : P.FG)
  let x : ↥(s : Set O) → O := Subtype.val
  have hx : ∀ i, x i ∈ P ^ 1 := fun i => by
    rw [pow_one, ← hs]
    exact Submodule.subset_span i.2
  have hspan : Ideal.span (Set.range x) = P := by
    rw [Subtype.range_coe]
    exact hs
  have hrange : Algebra.adjoin (O ⧸ P)
      (Set.range fun i => AssocGraded.mk P 1 (x i) (hx i)) = ⊤ := by
    rw [Algebra.adjoin_range_eq_range_aeval]
    exact (AlgHom.range_eq_top _).mpr (aeval_mk_surjective P x hx hspan)
  refine ⟨⟨⟨Finset.univ.image fun i => AssocGraded.mk P 1 (x i) (hx i), ?_⟩⟩, ?_⟩
  · rw [Finset.coe_image, Finset.coe_univ, Set.image_univ]
    exact hrange
  · rw [eq_top_iff, ← hrange]
    apply Algebra.adjoin_mono
    rintro _ ⟨i, rfl⟩
    exact Set.mem_iUnion.mpr ⟨0, AssocGraded.mk_mem_homogeneous P (hx i)⟩

open TensorProduct in
/-- `M ⊗_A A/I ≅ M/IM = gr⁰_I(M)`, so the two are flat over `A/I` together. -/
private theorem flat_quotient_tensor_iff_flat_grModPiece_zero {A : Type u} [CommRing A]
    (I : Ideal A) (M : Type u) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] :
    Module.Flat (A ⧸ I) ((A ⧸ I) ⊗[A] M) ↔ Module.Flat (A ⧸ I) (GrModPiece I M 0) := by
  have htop : I ^ 0 • (⊤ : Submodule A M) = ⊤ := by
    rw [pow_zero, Ideal.one_eq_top, Submodule.top_smul]
  let e₂ : ↥(I ^ 0 • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) ≃ₗ[A] M :=
    (LinearEquiv.ofEq _ _ htop).trans Submodule.topEquiv
  let e₃ : (M ⧸ (I • (⊤ : Submodule A M))) ≃ₗ[A] GrModPiece I M 0 :=
    Submodule.Quotient.equiv _ _ e₂.symm (by
      ext x
      rw [Submodule.mem_map_equiv, LinearEquiv.symm_symm, Submodule.mem_comap,
        Submodule.subtype_apply, zero_add, pow_one]
      rfl)
  exact Module.Flat.equiv_iff (((TensorProduct.quotTensorEquivQuotSMul M I).trans
    e₃).extendScalarsOfSurjective Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective)

private theorem isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_iff_grModMap_bijective {O : Type u} [CommRing O]
    [IsLocalRing O] [IsNoetherianRing O] (P : Ideal O) (hP : P ≠ ⊤) (I : Ideal (O ⧸ P))
    (hI : I ≠ ⊤) :
    IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal P ↔
      (Module.Flat ((O ⧸ P) ⧸ I) (GrModPiece I (AssocGraded P) 0) ∧
        ∀ m : ℕ, Function.Bijective (grModMap I (AssocGraded P) m)) := by
  have : Nontrivial (O ⧸ P) := Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mpr hP
  have : IsLocalRing (O ⧸ P) :=
    IsLocalRing.of_surjective' (Ideal.Quotient.mk P) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  have := assocGraded_gradedMonoid P
  let _ : DirectSum.Decomposition (AssocGraded.homogeneous P) :=
    (assocGraded_isInternal P).chooseDecomposition
  let _ : GradedAlgebra (AssocGraded.homogeneous P) := {}
  obtain ⟨hft, hgen⟩ := assocGraded_finiteType_and_adjoin P
  have hIj : I ≤ Ring.jacobson (O ⧸ P) := by
    rw [IsLocalRing.ringJacobson_eq_maximalIdeal]
    exact IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal hI
  have h := (flat_tfae_of_graded (O ⧸ P) (AssocGraded P) (AssocGraded.homogeneous P) hgen I
    hIj (AssocGraded P) (AssocGraded.homogeneous P)).out 1 3
  rw [isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_iff_isGrFlat, IsGrFlat, h,
    flat_quotient_tensor_iff_flat_grModPiece_zero]

/-- **The diagram of Theorem 2 for the double grades `(p, q)`** (pp. 195–196).  Assume (iii).  For
every `q`, the canonical `φ` of Lemma 2 of §1 for the `Ō`-module `gr^q_{P̄}(O)` and the ideal `Q̄'`
is bijective in every degree `p`; this is the homogeneous part of double grade `(p, q)` of
"`φ` is an isomorphism" (the diagram of p. 196):

"We want to prove that `φ` is an isomorphism.  We have the following commutative diagram of
canonical homomorphisms of doubly graded `O/Q̄`-algebras: [rows `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_P(M)) ⊗ gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)
→ gr_{Q̄}(gr_P(M))`, for `M = (J, R), R, O`], where the left vertical sequence is exact, the middle
horizontal arrow is isomorphic, and the other horizontal arrows `φ` and `φ'` are surjective.  It is
easy to see that if the right vertical sequence is proved exact, then `φ` is an isomorphism.  To
show that the right vertical sequence is exact, we consider the following commutative diagram for
each of their homogeneous parts with double grades `(p, q)`: [the `3 × 3` diagram with columns
`0 → Q̄ᵖ⁺¹ M → Q̄ᵖ M → grᵖ_{Q̄}(M) → 0` for `M = gr^q_P(J, R), gr^q_P(R), gr^q_{P̄}(O)`], where the
vertical sequences are all exact, `δ'` and `δ''` are injective, `ε'` and `ε''` are surjective.  It
is clear that `ε` is surjective.  In order to prove `Ker(ε) = Im(δ)` for this diagram, it suffices
that `Ker(ε') = Im(δ')`.  The homomorphism `ε'` is induced by the canonical homomorphism of
`gr^q_P(R)` onto `gr^q_{P̄}(O)` whose kernel is equal to `gr^q_P(J, R)`, by Lemma 5.  Hence
`Ker(ε') = Q̄ᵖ gr^q_P(R) ∩ gr^q_P(J, R)`.  By virtue of Lemma 7, the assumption (iii) implies that
`Q̄ᵖ gr^q_P(R) ∩ gr^q_P(J, R) = Q̄ᵖ gr^q_P(J, R)`, which is `Im(δ')`."

In Lean the modules are `X = gr^q_P(R)` (`GrPiece P q`, free over `R/P`, hence over `Ō` through
`Ō ≅ R/P`, which makes the middle arrow `φ_X` bijective by Lemma 2 of §1), `L = gr^q_P(J, R) =
Ker π` and `M = gr^q_{P̄}(O)`, `π : X → M` being the arrow of Lemma 5 in degree `q`
(`GrQuotDiagram.isShortExact_grPiece`).  The columns are `GrModPiece` (their exactness is the
definition of `GrModPiece`), `Ker(ε) = Im(δ)` is `GrModPiece.exact_map_of_inf_le` (the printed
reduction to `Ker(ε') = Im(δ')`), the surjectivity of the arrows `φ` for `L` and `M` is
`grModMap_surjective`, and "if the right vertical sequence is exact then `φ` is an isomorphism" is
`injective_grModMap_of_exact`.

The input `Ker(ε') = Im(δ')` is Lemma 7 (`inf_grIdeal_eq_mul_span_initialForm`) for `gr_P(R)`:
`Q̄ⁿ gr_P(R) ∩ gr_P(J, R) = Q̄ⁿ gr_P(J, R)`, in the homogeneous part of degree `q`.

Deviation from the paper (gap filled): Lemma 7 is an equality of ideals of the algebra `gr_P(R)`,
and the paper uses it for the homogeneous parts "by virtue of Lemma 7" without a word; the passage
to the part of degree `q` is made through the projection `gr_P(R) → gr^q_P(R)`, which is
`Ō`-linear and maps `Q̄ⁿ gr_P(R)` into `Q̄ⁿ gr^q_P(R)` and `gr_P(J, R)` into `gr^q_P(J, R)`
(`hHq`). -/
@[blueprint "lem:II.2-2-phi-grPiece"
  (statement := /-- In the notation of \cref{thm:II.2-2}, assume (iii) of that theorem. Then for all
    $p,q\ge0$ the canonical homomorphism $\varphi_{p,q}\colon\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}
    (\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)
    \to\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O))$ (the homogeneous part of double
    grade $(p,q)$ of $\varphi$, \cref{def:II.1-canonical-phi}) is bijective. (Theorem 2, pp.~195--196.) -/)
  (proof := /-- Consider the commutative diagram with columns $0\to\bar Q^{p+1}M\to\bar Q^pM\to
    \operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar Q}(M)\to0$ for $M=\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)$,
    $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)$, $\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O)$ (p.~196). The arrow
    $\varepsilon'\colon\bar Q^p\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)\to\bar Q^p\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O)$ is
    induced by the canonical homomorphism of $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)$ onto
    $\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O)$ whose kernel is $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)$ by
    \cref{lem:II.2-5-degree}, so $\operatorname{Ker}(\varepsilon')=\bar Q^p\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)\cap
    \operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)$, which by \cref{lem:II.2-7} (assumption (iii)) equals $\bar Q^p
    \operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)=\operatorname{Im}(\delta')$. By \cref{lem:II.2-2-right-exact} the right
    column $\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R))\to\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar Q}
    (\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R))\to\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O))$ is
    exact. The map $\varphi$ for the free (flat) $\bar O$-module $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)$ is an
    isomorphism (\cref{lem:II.1-2}), and the maps $\varphi$ for $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)$ and
    $\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O)$ are surjective, so $\varphi_{p,q}$ is bijective by
    \cref{lem:II.2-2-phi-injective}. -/)
  (latexEnv := "lemma")]
theorem bijective_grModMap_grPiece_of_hasInitialFormSystem (hJP : J ≤ P) (hPQ : P ≤ Q)
    (hsys : HasInitialFormSystem J P Q) (q m : ℕ) :
    Function.Bijective (grModMap Q̄' (GrPiece P̄ q) m) := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨k, f, hf, h₁, h₂⟩ := hsys
  obtain ⟨key, hH⟩ := inf_grIdeal_eq_mul_span_initialForm J P Q hJP hPQ f hf h₁ h₂
  let e : Ō ≃+* R ⧸ P := DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotOfLE hJP
  let _ : Module Ō (AssocGraded P) := Module.compHom _ e.toRingHom
  let _ : Module Ō (GrPiece P q) := Module.compHom _ e.toRingHom
  have hsmulA : ∀ (a : Ō) (g : AssocGraded P), a • g = e a • g := fun _ _ => rfl
  have hsmulX : ∀ (a : Ō) (x : GrPiece P q), a • x = e a • x := fun _ _ => rfl
  -- `gr^q_P(R)` is free over `R/P`, hence over `Ō`
  have hfreeP : Module.Free (R ⧸ P) (GrPiece P q) :=
    isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_of_isRegularLocalRing_quotient P q
  have hflat : Module.Flat Ō (GrPiece P q) := by
    have : Module.Free Ō (GrPiece P q) :=
      Module.Free.of_basis ((Module.Free.chooseBasis (R ⧸ P) (GrPiece P q)).mapCoeffs e.symm
        (fun c y => by show e (e.symm c) • y = c • y; rw [e.apply_symm_apply]))
    infer_instance
  -- the `Ō`-linear maps: `π_w : gr_P(R) → gr_{P̄}(O)`, `π : gr^q_P(R) → gr^q_{P̄}(O)`, and the
  -- inclusion `ι` and projection `ρ` between `gr^q_P(R)` and `gr_P(R)`
  have htowA : IsScalarTower R Ō (AssocGraded P) :=
    IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_smul fun r g => by
      rw [hsmulA]
      exact algebraMap_smul (R ⧸ P) r g
  have htowX : IsScalarTower R Ō (GrPiece P q) :=
    IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_smul fun r x => by
      rw [hsmulX]
      exact algebraMap_smul (R ⧸ P) r x
  have htowB : IsScalarTower R Ō (AssocGraded P̄) :=
    IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_smul (R := R) (A := Ō) (M := AssocGraded P̄) fun r g => by
      show (algebraMap 𝒪 Ō (algebraMap R 𝒪 r)) • g = r • g
      rw [algebraMap_smul Ō (algebraMap R 𝒪 r) g, algebraMap_smul 𝒪 r g]
  have hsurj : Function.Surjective (algebraMap R Ō) :=
    Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective.comp Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  let πw : AssocGraded P →ₗ[Ō] AssocGraded P̄ :=
    LinearMap.extendScalarsOfSurjective hsurj (grMapQuot P J).toLinearMap
  let π : GrPiece P q →ₗ[Ō] GrPiece P̄ q :=
    LinearMap.extendScalarsOfSurjective hsurj (GrQuotDiagram.grPieceMap P J q)
  let ι : GrPiece P q →ₗ[Ō] AssocGraded P :=
    LinearMap.extendScalarsOfSurjective hsurj
      ((AssocGraded.ofGrPiece P q).restrictScalars R)
  let ρ : AssocGraded P →ₗ[Ō] GrPiece P q :=
    LinearMap.extendScalarsOfSurjective hsurj ((grProj P q).restrictScalars R)
  have hπι : ∀ x, πw (ι x) = AssocGraded.ofGrPiece P̄ q (π x) := fun x =>
    GrQuotDiagram.grMapQuot_ofGrPiece P J q x
  have hρπ : ∀ g, π (ρ g) = grProj P̄ q (πw g) := fun g =>
    (GrQuotDiagram.grProj_grMapQuot P J q g).symm
  have hπker : ∀ g, g ∈ LinearMap.ker πw ↔ g ∈ grIdeal P J := fun g => by
    rw [LinearMap.mem_ker, ← ker_grMapQuot, RingHom.mem_ker]
    rfl
  have hπsurj : Function.Surjective π := (GrQuotDiagram.isShortExact_grPiece P J q).surjective
  -- Lemma 7 for the whole algebra `gr_P(R)`: `Q̄'ⁿ gr_P(R) ∩ gr_P(J, R) = Q̄'ⁿ gr_P(J, R)`
  let φ : Ō →+* AssocGraded P := (algebraMap (R ⧸ P) (AssocGraded P)).comp e.toRingHom
  have hφsmul : ∀ (a : Ō) (g : AssocGraded P), a • g = φ a * g := fun a g => by
    show e a • g = _
    exact Algebra.smul_def _ _
  have hQφ : (Q̄'.map φ) = Q.map (algebraMap R (AssocGraded P)) := by
    have : Q̄' = Q.map (algebraMap R Ō) := by
      rw [Ideal.map_map]
      rfl
    rw [this, Ideal.map_map]
    congr 1
  have hHw : ∀ n : ℕ, ((Q̄' ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule Ō (AssocGraded P))) ⊓ LinearMap.ker πw) ≤
      Q̄' ^ n • LinearMap.ker πw := by
    intro n x hx
    obtain ⟨hx1, hx2⟩ := hx
    have hxQ : x ∈ (Q.map (algebraMap R (AssocGraded P))) ^ n := by
      refine Submodule.smul_induction_on hx1 (fun a ha g _ => ?_) (fun _ _ h h' => add_mem h h')
      rw [hφsmul, ← hQφ, ← Ideal.map_pow]
      exact Ideal.mul_mem_right _ _ (Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ ha)
    have hxJ : x ∈ grIdeal P J := (hπker x).mp hx2
    have hx3 := (key n).le ⟨hxQ, hxJ⟩
    rw [hH.symm] at hx3
    rw [← hQφ, ← Ideal.map_pow] at hx3
    refine Submodule.mul_induction_on hx3 (fun u hu v hv => ?_) (fun _ _ h h' => add_mem h h')
    have hC : ∀ u ∈ Ideal.map φ (Q̄' ^ n), ∀ v ∈ grIdeal P J,
        u * v ∈ (Q̄' ^ n • LinearMap.ker πw : Submodule Ō (AssocGraded P)) := by
      intro u hu
      induction hu using Submodule.span_induction with
      | mem u hu' =>
        obtain ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩ := hu'
        intro v hv
        rw [← hφsmul]
        exact Submodule.smul_mem_smul ha ((hπker v).mpr hv)
      | zero =>
        intro v _
        rw [zero_mul]
        exact zero_mem _
      | add u₁ u₂ _ _ h₁ h₂ =>
        intro v hv
        rw [add_mul]
        exact add_mem (h₁ v hv) (h₂ v hv)
      | smul c u _ h =>
        intro v hv
        rw [smul_eq_mul, mul_comm c u, mul_assoc]
        exact h _ (Ideal.mul_mem_left _ c hv)
    exact hC u hu v hv
  -- the same for the homogeneous part of degree `q`: `Ker(ε') = Im(δ')`
  have hHq : ∀ n : ℕ, ∀ x ∈ Q̄' ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule Ō (GrPiece P q)), π x = 0 →
      x ∈ (Q̄' ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule Ō (LinearMap.ker π))).map (LinearMap.ker π).subtype := by
    intro n x hx hπx
    have h1 : ι x ∈ Q̄' ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule Ō (AssocGraded P)) :=
      Submodule.smul_top_le_comap_smul_top (Q̄' ^ n) ι hx
    have h2 : ι x ∈ LinearMap.ker πw := by
      rw [LinearMap.mem_ker, hπι, hπx, map_zero]
    have h4 : ρ (ι x) ∈ (Q̄' ^ n • LinearMap.ker πw).map ρ :=
      Submodule.mem_map_of_mem (hHw n ⟨h1, h2⟩)
    rw [Submodule.map_smul''] at h4
    have h5 : ρ (ι x) = x := grProj_ofGrPiece_same P q x
    have h6 : (LinearMap.ker πw).map ρ ≤ LinearMap.ker π := by
      rintro _ ⟨g, hg, rfl⟩
      have hg' : πw g = 0 := hg
      show π (ρ g) = 0
      rw [hρπ, hg', map_zero]
    rw [h5] at h4
    rw [Submodule.map_smul'', Submodule.map_top, Submodule.range_subtype]
    exact Submodule.smul_mono le_rfl h6 h4
  -- the diagram of p. 196 for the double grades `(p, q)`, `p = m`
  have hinj : Function.Injective (grModMap Q̄' (GrPiece P̄ q) m) :=
    injective_grModMap_of_exact Q̄' (LinearMap.ker π).subtype π hπsurj (fun x => x.2) m
      (grModMap_injective_of_flat Q̄' (GrPiece P q) m) (grModMap_surjective Q̄' _ m)
      (GrModPiece.exact_map_of_inf_le Q̄' (LinearMap.ker π).subtype π hπsurj (fun x => x.2) m
        (hHq m))
  exact ⟨hinj, grModMap_surjective Q̄' _ m⟩

/-- (iii) ⟹ the canonical `φ` of (iv) is an isomorphism (p. 196: "We want to prove that `φ` is an
isomorphism"): `φ` is the direct sum over the double grades `(p, q)` of the maps `φ_{pq}`
(`bijective_grModMap_assocGraded`, Corollary to Lemma 2 of §1, p. 182), each of which is bijective
by the diagram of p. 196 (`bijective_grModMap_grPiece_of_hasInitialFormSystem`). -/
private theorem grModMap_bijective_of_hasInitialFormSystem (hJP : J ≤ P) (hPQ : P ≤ Q)
    (hsys : HasInitialFormSystem J P Q) (m : ℕ) :
    Function.Bijective (grModMap Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m) :=
  bijective_grModMap_assocGraded P̄ Q̄' m fun q =>
    bijective_grModMap_grPiece_of_hasInitialFormSystem J P Q hJP hPQ hsys q m

omit [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P)] [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ Q)] in
open TensorProduct in
/-- **Flatness of `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` from `ψ'`** (p. 195): "First of all, the isomorphism `ψ'`
of (ii) induces isomorphisms of `O/Q̄`-modules
`⊕_{p+q=n} gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)` for all non-negative
integers `n`, where `⊕` signifies the direct sum.  By the assumption that `gr_{Q̄}(O)` is flat over
`O/Q̄`, `grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)` must be a free `O/Q̄`-module, and hence the direct summands
`gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` are such also (see Lemma 1 of §1).  Note that
`gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` are free `O/Q̄`-modules.  Therefore
`gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is flat over `O/Q̄`."

The direct summands `E_{pq} = gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗ gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` are free, for all
`(p, q)`, by Lemma 1 of §1 (`free_psiSummand_of_bijective_psiDeg`).  "Therefore" is read with the
summand `q = 0`: `E_{p,0} = gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗ gr⁰_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` and
`gr⁰_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) = O/Q̄`, so that `gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O))` is free over `O/Q̄`
(`free_grModPiece_zero_grPiece_of_bijective_psiDeg`).

Deviation from the paper (gap filled): the paper does not say which of the free modules
`gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` (all of positive rank) it uses in "Therefore" (here `q = 0`, of rank one), and it
passes without a word from the pieces `grᵖ_{P̄}(O)` to `gr_{P̄}(O) = ⊕ₚ grᵖ_{P̄}(O)` and from `O/Q̄`
to `Ō/Q̄' ≅ O/Q̄`; these steps are made explicit (`free_of_addEquiv_of_ringEquiv`,
`exists_addEquiv_grModPiece_zero_map`, `TensorProduct.directSumRight`). -/
theorem flat_grModPiece_zero_of_bijective_psiDeg (hJP : J ≤ P) (hPQ : P ≤ Q)
    (hQflat : IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal Q̄) {s : ℕ} (y : Fin s → R)
    (hQY : Q = P ⊔ Ideal.span (Set.range y))
    (hqr : IsQuasiRegular fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk P (y j))
    (hbij : ∀ n, Function.Bijective (psiDeg P̄ Q̄ (Ideal.map_mono hPQ)
      (fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk J (y j)) (isQuasiRegular_map_quotient J P y hJP hqr)
      (span_map_mk_eq_map_quotient J P Q y hQY) n)) :
    Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0) := by
  classical
  have hJM : J ≤ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R :=
    hJP.trans (IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal Ideal.IsPrime.ne_top')
  have : IsLocalRing 𝒪 := isLocalRing_quotient_of_le hJM
  have hQbar : Q̄.IsPrime := Ideal.map_isPrime_of_surjective Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
    (by rw [Ideal.mk_ker]; exact hJP.trans hPQ)
  have : IsLocalRing (𝒪 ⧸ Q̄) :=
    IsLocalRing.of_surjective' (Ideal.Quotient.mk Q̄) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  have hPQ' : P̄ ≤ Q̄ := Ideal.map_mono hPQ
  -- the pieces `gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O))` are free over `O/Q̄`, by `ψ'` (the summands `E_{p,0}`)
  have hpiece : ∀ p, Module.Free (𝒪 ⧸ Q̄) (GrModPiece Q̄ (GrPiece P̄ p) 0) := fun p =>
    free_grModPiece_zero_grPiece_of_bijective_psiDeg P̄ Q̄ hPQ' _
      (isQuasiRegular_map_quotient J P y hJP hqr) (span_map_mk_eq_map_quotient J P Q y hQY)
      hQbar.ne_top hQflat p (hbij p)
  -- hence free, in particular flat, over `Ō/Q̄'`
  have hpiece' : ∀ p, Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (GrPiece P̄ p) 0) := fun p => by
    obtain ⟨f, hf⟩ := exists_addEquiv_grModPiece_zero_map P̄ Q̄ hPQ' (GrPiece P̄ p)
    have : Module.Free (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (GrPiece P̄ p) 0) :=
      free_of_addEquiv_of_ringEquiv (DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotOfLE hPQ') f hf
    exact Module.Flat.of_free
  have htensor : ∀ p, Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') ((Ō ⧸ Q̄') ⊗[Ō] GrPiece P̄ p) := fun p =>
    (flat_quotient_tensor_iff_flat_grModPiece_zero Q̄' (GrPiece P̄ p)).mpr (hpiece' p)
  -- `K ⊗ gr_{P̄}(O) = ⨁ₚ K ⊗ grᵖ_{P̄}(O)`
  have hsum : Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄')
      (DirectSum ℕ fun p => (Ō ⧸ Q̄') ⊗[Ō] GrPiece P̄ p) := inferInstance
  let e₁ : (Ō ⧸ Q̄') ⊗[Ō] (DirectSum ℕ fun p => GrPiece P̄ p) ≃ₗ[Ō ⧸ Q̄']
      DirectSum ℕ fun p => (Ō ⧸ Q̄') ⊗[Ō] GrPiece P̄ p :=
    TensorProduct.directSumRight Ō (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (fun p => GrPiece P̄ p)
  let e₂ : (DirectSum ℕ fun p => GrPiece P̄ p) ≃ₗ[Ō] AssocGraded P̄ :=
    LinearEquiv.ofBijective (toGr P̄) (toGr_bijective P̄)
  let e₃ : (Ō ⧸ Q̄') ⊗[Ō] (DirectSum ℕ fun p => GrPiece P̄ p) ≃ₗ[Ō ⧸ Q̄']
      (Ō ⧸ Q̄') ⊗[Ō] AssocGraded P̄ :=
    TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.congr (LinearEquiv.refl (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (Ō ⧸ Q̄')) e₂
  have : Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') ((Ō ⧸ Q̄') ⊗[Ō] AssocGraded P̄) :=
    (Module.Flat.equiv_iff (e₁.symm.trans e₃)).mp hsum
  exact (flat_quotient_tensor_iff_flat_grModPiece_zero Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄)).mp this

/-- **(ii) and (iii) together imply (iv)** of Theorem 2 (p. 195), the last step of the paper's
proof, as printed (pp. 195–196): the isomorphism `ψ'` of (ii) exhibits the
`E_{pq} = gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗ gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` as direct summands of the free modules
`grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)`, hence they are free (Lemma 1 of §1, `free_psiSummand_of_bijective_psiDeg`), whence the
flatness of `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` (`flat_grModPiece_zero_of_bijective_psiDeg`); and `φ` is an
isomorphism by the doubly graded diagram of p. 196, whose homogeneous parts of double grade
`(p, q)` are treated in `bijective_grModMap_grPiece_of_hasInitialFormSystem` (Lemma 5 degreewise,
Lemma 7, `GrModPiece.exact_map_of_inf_le`, `injective_grModMap_of_exact`), `φ` being the direct sum
of these (`grModMap_bijective_of_hasInitialFormSystem`).  There is no deviation in the route; the
steps the paper does not write are filled in as listed in the docstrings of the lemmas used. -/
theorem flat_and_bijective_grModMap_of_bijective_psiDeg (hJP : J ≤ P) (hPQ : P ≤ Q)
    (hQflat : IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal Q̄) {s : ℕ} (y : Fin s → R)
    (hQY : Q = P ⊔ Ideal.span (Set.range y))
    (hqr : IsQuasiRegular fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk P (y j))
    (hbij : ∀ n, Function.Bijective (psiDeg P̄ Q̄ (Ideal.map_mono hPQ)
      (fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk J (y j)) (isQuasiRegular_map_quotient J P y hJP hqr)
      (span_map_mk_eq_map_quotient J P Q y hQY) n))
    (hsys : HasInitialFormSystem J P Q) :
    Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0) ∧
      ∀ m : ℕ, Function.Bijective (grModMap Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m) :=
  ⟨flat_grModPiece_zero_of_bijective_psiDeg J P Q hJP hPQ hQflat y hQY hqr hbij,
    grModMap_bijective_of_hasInitialFormSystem J P Q hJP hPQ hsys⟩

/-- Theorem 2, p. 195: "Let `R` be a regular local ring and `J` an ideal in `R`. Let `P` and `Q`
be prime ideals in `R` such that `Q ⊇ P ⊇ J` and that `R/P` and `R/Q` are both regular. Let
`O = R/J`, `Q̄ = Q/J`, and `P̄ = P/J`. Then the following conditions are equivalent to each
other:
(i) `gr_{P̄}(O)` is flat over `O/P̄`.
(ii) `gr_{Q̄}(O)` is flat over `O/Q̄` and the above homomorphism of graded `O/Q̄`-algebras
`ψ' : gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → gr_{Q̄}(O)` is isomorphic.
(iii) `gr_{Q̄}(O)` is flat over `O/Q̄`, and there exists a system of elements `(f₁, …, f_m)` of
`J` such that (1) `ν_Q(f_j) = ν_P(f_j)` for `1 ≤ j ≤ m`, and (2) if `φ_j` denotes the initial
form of `f_j` in `gr_Q(R)` for `1 ≤ j ≤ m`, then `gr_Q(J, R) = (φ₁, …, φ_m) gr_Q(R)`.
(iv) `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is flat over `O/Q̄`, and the canonical homomorphism of graded
`O/Q̄`-algebras `φ : gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → gr_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is
isomorphic."

Footnote 42: "The assumption that `O` is given as a homomorphic image of a regular local ring
`R` is only necessary for (iii). The equivalence among the other three conditions holds
without this assumption. (cf. Proposition 1, §1.) In fact, we can easily reduce the proof of
the equivalence (without the assumption) to the case in which `O` is complete, hence, it is a
homomorphic image of a formal power series ring over a discrete valuation ring of rank one (or,
rank zero)."

Deviation from the paper (statement modified): **condition (ii) is omitted from this statement**
(see the module docstring): `ψ'` depends on the choice of `(y₁, …, y_s)`, so this statement is the
equivalence `(i) ⟺ (iii) ⟺ (iv)`; with (ii) the theorem is `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_psiDeg`
(Lemma 8 is `bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem`).  Footnote 42 is not formalized.

Proof (as in the paper, p. 195): "By Proposition 1 of § 1, (i) ⟹ (ii). By Lemma 8, (ii) ⟺ (iii).
By corollary to Lemma 2 of § 1, (iv) ⟹ (i). Thus it suffices to prove that (ii) and (iii)
together imply (iv)."  Since (ii) is omitted from this statement, it enters only as the
intermediate step, for a system `y` with `Q = P + (y)` and quasi-regular `ȳ_j` as on pp. 183 and
193 (`exists_adapted_lift`): (i) ⟹ (ii) is Proposition 1 of §1
(`psiDeg_bijective_of_isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal`, with `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_of_le` for the
flatness of `gr_{Q̄}(O)`), (ii) ⟺ (iii) is Lemma 8 (`bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem`),
(ii) + (iii) ⟹ (iv) is the paper's last step (`flat_and_bijective_grModMap_of_bijective_psiDeg`:
the isomorphism `ψ'` exhibits the `gr⁰_{Q̄}(grᵖ_{P̄}(O)) ⊗ gr^q_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄)` as direct summands
of `grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)`, whence the flatness, and `φ` is an isomorphism by Lemma 5 and Lemma 7), and
(iv) ⟹ (i) is the corollary to Lemma 2 of §1.

Proof of (ii) + (iii) ⟹ (iv), as printed (pp. 195–196; the same as in the paper-form node
`isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_psiDeg_of_span_eq_map`).  The direct summands `E_{pq}` of the free
modules `grⁿ_{Q̄}(O)` are free for every `(p, q)` by Lemma 1 of §1
(`free_psiSummand_of_bijective_psiDeg`), and `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is flat
(`flat_grModPiece_zero_of_bijective_psiDeg`).  The canonical `φ` is an isomorphism by the doubly
graded diagram of p. 196, for each double grade `(p, q)` (`bijective_grModMap_grPiece_of_hasInitialFormSystem`):
the middle arrow is an isomorphism because `gr^q_P(R)` is free over `R/P` (Lemma 2 of §1), the other
arrows are surjective (`grModMap_surjective`), "if the right vertical sequence is exact then `φ` is
an isomorphism" is `injective_grModMap_of_exact`, "it suffices that `Ker(ε') = Im(δ')`" is
`GrModPiece.exact_map_of_inf_le`, `ε'` is the arrow of Lemma 5 in degree `q`
(`GrQuotDiagram.isShortExact_grPiece`) and `Ker(ε') = Im(δ')` is Lemma 7 for the homogeneous
part of degree `q`; and `φ` is the direct sum of the maps of double grade `(p, q)`
(`bijective_grModMap_assocGraded`).

Deviation from the paper (gap filled): "Therefore `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is flat over `O/Q̄`"
(p. 195) passes from the pieces `grᵖ_{P̄}(O)` to the whole algebra `gr_{P̄}(O) = ⨁ₚ grᵖ_{P̄}(O)`,
from `gr⁰` for `Q̄` over `O/Q̄` to `gr⁰` for `Q̄'` over `Ō/Q̄'`, and does not say which summand
`q` it uses (here `q = 0`), without a word; all are made explicit
(`flat_grModPiece_zero_of_bijective_psiDeg`).  "It is easy to see that if the right vertical
sequence is proved exact, then `φ` is an isomorphism", "It is clear that `ε` is surjective", "In
order to prove `Ker(ε) = Im(δ)` for this diagram, it suffices that `Ker(ε') = Im(δ')`", "By virtue
of Lemma 7 ... `Q̄ᵖ gr^q_P(R) ∩ gr^q_P(J,R) = Q̄ᵖ gr^q_P(J,R)`" (Lemma 7 is an equality of ideals of
`gr_P(R)`, used for its homogeneous parts) and "`φ` is the direct sum of the `φ_{pq}`" are unargued
steps, proved (`injective_grModMap_of_exact`, `GrModPiece.exact_map_of_inf_le`,
`bijective_grModMap_grPiece_of_hasInitialFormSystem`, `bijective_grModMap_assocGraded`).

Formalization notes (encoding).
* (i) is `IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal P̄` (`O` is a noetherian local ring; Def. 1 of Ch. 0 and
  Lemma 1 of §1); the first half of (iii) is `IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal Q̄` and its second half
  is `HasInitialFormSystem J P Q`.
* In (iv), `gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is `GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0`, the paper's `O/Q̄` is
  identified with `Ō/Q̄'` (`Ō = O/P̄`) via the canonical isomorphism `O/Q̄ ≅ (O/P̄)/(Q̄/P̄)`, and
  "`φ` is isomorphic" is `∀ m, Function.Bijective (grModMap Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m)`, `φ` being
  the canonical map of Lemma 2 of §1 for the `Ō`-module `gr_{P̄}(O)` and the ideal `Q̄'`
  (`def:II.1-canonical-phi`). -/
@[blueprint "thm:II.2-2"
  (statement := /-- Let $R$ be a regular local ring and $J$ an ideal in $R$. Let $P$ and $Q$
    be prime ideals in $R$ such that $Q\supseteq P\supseteq J$ and that $R/P$ and $R/Q$ are
    both regular. Let $O=R/J$, $\bar Q=Q/J$, and $\bar P=P/J$. Then the following conditions
    are equivalent to each other:
    (i) $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O)$ is flat over $O/\bar P$
    (\cref{def:normally-flat-ideal}).
    (ii) $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$ is flat over $O/\bar Q$ and the above homomorphism
    of graded $O/\bar Q$-algebras $\psi'\colon\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}
    (\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar P}
    (O/\bar P)\to\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$ is isomorphic.
    (iii) $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$ is flat over $O/\bar Q$, and there exists a system
    of elements $(f_1,\dots,f_m)$ of $J$ such that (1) $\nu_Q(f_j)=\nu_P(f_j)$ for
    $1\le j\le m$, and (2) if $\varphi_j$ denotes the initial form of $f_j$ in
    $\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$ for $1\le j\le m$, then $\operatorname{gr}_Q(J,R)=
    (\varphi_1,\dots,\varphi_m)\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$ (\cref{def:II.2-initial-form-system}).
    (iv) $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))$ is flat over
    $O/\bar Q$, and the canonical homomorphism of graded $O/\bar Q$-algebras
    $\varphi\colon\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}
    \operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)\to\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}
    (\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))$ (\cref{def:II.1-canonical-phi}, for the
    $O/\bar P$-module $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O)$ and the ideal $\bar Q/\bar P$) is
    isomorphic. (Theorem 2, p.~195.)

    Footnote 42: ``The assumption that $O$ is given as a homomorphic image of a regular local
    ring $R$ is only necessary for (iii). The equivalence among the other three conditions
    holds without this assumption. (cf. Proposition 1, \S1.)''

    \textbf{Deviation from the paper (statement modified).} The homomorphism $\psi'$ of
    p.~193 depends on the choice of a system $(y_1,\dots,y_s)$; condition (ii) is therefore
    omitted from this statement, which is the equivalence of (i), (iii) and (iv), and is
    restored in \cref{thm:II.2-2-psi}.

    \textbf{Not formalized.} Footnote~42 is not formalized.

    Formalization note (encoding): in (iv) the paper's $O/\bar Q$ is identified with
    $(O/\bar P)/(\bar Q/\bar P)$, and ``$\varphi$ is isomorphic'' means that each homogeneous
    component (in the $\bar Q$-degree) of $\varphi$ is bijective. -/)
  (proof := /-- By \cref{prop:II.1-1} of \S1, (i)$\Rightarrow$(ii). By Lemma 8,
    (ii)$\Leftrightarrow$(iii). By \cref{cor:II.1-lem2} of \S1, (iv)$\Rightarrow$(i). Thus it
    suffices to prove that (ii) and (iii) together imply (iv). First of all, the isomorphism
    $\psi'$ of (ii) induces isomorphisms of $O/\bar Q$-modules
    $\sum_{p+q=n}\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}
    \operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)\to\operatorname{gr}^n_{\bar Q}(O)$ for all
    non-negative integers $n$, where $\sum$ signifies the direct sum. By the assumption that
    $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$ is flat over $O/\bar Q$, $\operatorname{gr}^n_{\bar Q}(O)$
    must be a free $(O/\bar Q)$-module, and hence the direct summands
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}
    \operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)$ are such also (see \cref{lem:II.1-1}).
    Note that $\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)$ are free $(O/\bar Q)$-modules.
    Therefore $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))$ is flat over
    $O/\bar Q$. We want to prove that $\varphi$ is an isomorphism. We have the commutative
    diagram of canonical homomorphisms of doubly graded $O/\bar Q$-algebras (p.~196) with rows
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_P(J,R))\otimes\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar
    P}(O/\bar P)\to\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_P(J,R))$,
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_P(R))\otimes\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar
    P}(O/\bar P)\to\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_P(R))$ and
    $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))\otimes\operatorname{gr}_{\bar
    Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)\xrightarrow{\varphi}\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar
    P}(O))$, where the left vertical sequence is exact, the middle horizontal arrow is
    isomorphic, and the other horizontal arrows $\varphi$ and $\varphi'$ are surjective. It is
    easy to see that if the right vertical sequence is proved exact, then $\varphi$ is an
    isomorphism. To show that the right vertical sequence is exact, we consider for each pair
    of double grades $(p,q)$ the commutative diagram with exact columns
    $0\to Q^{p+1}\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)\to Q^p\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)\to
    \operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R))\to0$, and similarly for
    $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)$ and $\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O)$, in which $\zeta'$,
    $\zeta''$ are injective and $\varepsilon'$, $\varepsilon''$ surjective. It is clear that
    $\varepsilon$ is surjective. In order to prove $\operatorname{Ker}(\varepsilon)=
    \operatorname{Im}(\zeta)$ for this diagram, it suffices that
    $\operatorname{Ker}(\varepsilon')=\operatorname{Im}(\zeta')$. The homomorphism
    $\varepsilon'$ is induced by the canonical homomorphism of $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)$ onto
    $\operatorname{gr}^q_{\bar P}(O)$ whose kernel is equal to $\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)$,
    by \cref{lem:II.2-5}. Hence $\operatorname{Ker}(\varepsilon')=Q^p\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)
    \cap\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)$. By virtue of \cref{lem:II.2-7}, the assumption (iii)
    implies that $Q^p\operatorname{gr}^q_P(R)\cap\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)=
    Q^p\operatorname{gr}^q_P(J,R)$, which is $\operatorname{Im}(\zeta')$.

    \textbf{Proof in Lean.} Since (ii) is omitted from the statement, it enters only as the
    intermediate step: a system $(y_j)$ with $Q=P+(y)$ and $\bar y_j$ part of a regular system of
    parameters of $R/P$ is chosen as on pp.~183 and~193, (i)$\Rightarrow$(ii) is
    \cref{prop:II.1-1-psi}, (ii)$\Leftrightarrow$(iii) is Lemma~8
    (\cref{lem:II.2-8-quasi-regular}), (ii)$+$(iii)$\Rightarrow$(iv) is the argument above, and
    (iv)$\Rightarrow$(i) is \cref{cor:II.1-lem2}.

    \textbf{Deviation from the paper (gap filled).} ``Therefore $\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar
    Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))$ is flat over $O/\bar Q$'' (p.~195): the passage from the
    pieces $\operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar P}(O)$ to $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O)=\bigoplus_p
    \operatorname{gr}^p_{\bar P}(O)$, and from $\operatorname{gr}^0$ for $\bar Q$ over $O/\bar Q$
    to $\operatorname{gr}^0$ for $\bar Q'$ over $\bar O/\bar Q'\cong O/\bar Q$, and the choice of
    the summand ($q=0$) are tacit in the paper and proved; likewise the steps ``it is clear that
    $\varepsilon$ is surjective'', ``it suffices that $\operatorname{Ker}(\varepsilon')=
    \operatorname{Im}(\zeta')$'', ``it is easy to see that if the right vertical sequence is proved
    exact, then $\varphi$ is an isomorphism'', the use of Lemma~7 (an equality of ideals of
    $\operatorname{gr}_P(R)$) for the homogeneous parts, and that $\varphi$ is the direct sum of the
    maps of double grade $(p,q)$ (\cref{lem:II.2-2-phi-grPiece}, \cref{lem:II.2-2-right-exact},
    \cref{lem:II.2-2-phi-injective}). -/)
  (latexEnv := "theorem")]
theorem isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae (hJP : J ≤ P) (hPQ : P ≤ Q) :
    [IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal P̄,
      IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal Q̄ ∧ HasInitialFormSystem J P Q,
      Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0) ∧
        ∀ m : ℕ, Function.Bijective (grModMap Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m)].TFAE := by
  have hJM : J ≤ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R :=
    hJP.trans (IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal Ideal.IsPrime.ne_top')
  have : IsLocalRing 𝒪 := isLocalRing_quotient_of_le hJM
  have hPbar : P̄.IsPrime := Ideal.map_isPrime_of_surjective Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
    (by rw [Ideal.mk_ker]; exact hJP)
  have hQbar : Q̄.IsPrime := Ideal.map_isPrime_of_surjective Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
    (by rw [Ideal.mk_ker]; exact hJP.trans hPQ)
  have hQbar' : Q̄'.IsPrime := Ideal.map_isPrime_of_surjective Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
    (by rw [Ideal.mk_ker]; exact Ideal.map_mono hPQ)
  have : IsRegularLocalRing (𝒪 ⧸ P̄) :=
    IsRegularLocalRing.of_ringEquiv (DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotOfLE hJP).symm
  have : IsRegularLocalRing (𝒪 ⧸ Q̄) :=
    IsRegularLocalRing.of_ringEquiv (DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotOfLE (hJP.trans hPQ)).symm
  -- the system `(y₁, …, y_s)` of pp. 183 and 193: a part of a regular system of parameters of
  -- `R/P` generating `Q/P`, lifted to `R`, so that `Q = P + (y)`
  obtain ⟨s, y, hqr, -, hQY, -⟩ := exists_adapted_lift P Q hPQ
  -- the paper's proof: "By Proposition 1 of § 1, (i) ⟹ (ii). By Lemma 8, (ii) ⟺ (iii). By
  -- corollary to Lemma 2 of § 1, (iv) ⟹ (i). Thus it suffices to prove that (ii) and (iii)
  -- together imply (iv)."  Condition (ii) is not part of this statement, so it enters as the
  -- bijectivity of `ψ'` (`psiDeg`) for the system `y`.
  -- (i) ⟹ (ii) by Proposition 1 of §1 (the flatness of `gr_{Q̄}(O)` is Proposition 1 (2)), and
  -- (ii) ⟹ (iii) by Lemma 8
  tfae_have 1 → 2 := fun h =>
    ⟨(isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_of_le P̄ Q̄ (Ideal.map_mono hPQ) h).2,
      (bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem J P Q y hJP hPQ hQY hqr).mp
        (psiDeg_bijective_of_isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal P̄ Q̄ (Ideal.map_mono hPQ)
          (fun j => Ideal.Quotient.mk J (y j)) _ _ h (map_eq_sup_span_map J P Q y hQY))⟩
  -- (iii) ⟹ (ii) by Lemma 8, and (ii) and (iii) together imply (iv): the last step of the proof
  tfae_have 2 → 3 := fun h =>
    flat_and_bijective_grModMap_of_bijective_psiDeg J P Q hJP hPQ h.1 y hQY hqr
      ((bijective_psiDeg_iff_hasInitialFormSystem J P Q y hJP hPQ hQY hqr).mpr h.2) h.2
  -- (iv) ⟹ (i): the corollary to Lemma 2 of §1
  tfae_have 3 → 1 := fun h =>
    (isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_iff_grModMap_bijective P̄ hPbar.ne_top Q̄' hQbar'.ne_top).mpr h
  tfae_finish

/-- Corollary 1 of Theorem 2, pp. 196–197: "The notation and assumptions being as in Theorem 2,
assume that `Q` is the maximal ideal of `R`. Then the following conditions are equivalent to
each other:
(i) `gr_{P̄}(O)` is flat over `O/P̄`.
(ii) The homomorphism of graded `O/Q̄`-algebras
`ψ' : gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → gr_{Q̄}(O)` is isomorphic.
(iii) There exists a system of elements `(f₁, …, f_m)` of `J` such that (1) `ν_Q(f_j) = ν_P(f_j)`
for `1 ≤ j ≤ m`, and (2) the initial forms of the `f_j` in `gr_Q(R)` generate the ideal
`gr_Q(J, R)`.
(iv) The canonical homomorphism of graded `O/Q̄`-algebras
`φ : gr⁰_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O)) ⊗_{O/Q̄} gr_{Q̄/P̄}(O/P̄) → gr_{Q̄}(gr_{P̄}(O))` is isomorphic."

Deviation from the paper (statement modified): as in Theorem 2, condition (ii) is omitted from
this statement (it is restored in `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_psiDeg_of_eq_maximalIdeal`).  Formalization note
(encoding): the encodings of (i), (iii), (iv) are those of `isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae`; "`Q` is the maximal ideal of
`R`" is the hypothesis `Q = maximalIdeal R` (the assumptions `Q` prime and `R/Q` regular of
Theorem 2 are then automatic but are kept, "the notation and assumptions being as in
Theorem 2"). -/
@[blueprint "cor:II.2-thm2-1"
  (statement := /-- The notation and assumptions being as in \cref{thm:II.2-2}, assume that
    $Q$ is the maximal ideal of $R$. Then the following conditions are equivalent to each
    other: (i) $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O)$ is flat over $O/\bar P$. (ii) The homomorphism
    of graded $O/\bar Q$-algebras $\psi'\colon\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{
    \bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)\to
    \operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(O)$ is isomorphic. (iii) There exists a system of elements
    $(f_1,\dots,f_m)$ of $J$ such that (1) $\nu_Q(f_j)=\nu_P(f_j)$ for $1\le j\le m$, and
    (2) the initial forms of the $f_j$ in $\operatorname{gr}_Q(R)$ generate the ideal
    $\operatorname{gr}_Q(J,R)$ (\cref{def:II.2-initial-form-system}). (iv) The canonical
    homomorphism of graded $O/\bar Q$-algebras $\varphi\colon\operatorname{gr}^0_{\bar Q}
    (\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))\otimes_{O/\bar Q}\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q/\bar P}(O/\bar P)
    \to\operatorname{gr}_{\bar Q}(\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O))$ is isomorphic.
    (Corollary 1, pp.~196--197.)

    \textbf{Deviation from the paper (statement modified).} As in \cref{thm:II.2-2}, condition
    (ii) is omitted from this statement, which is the equivalence of (i), (iii) and (iv); it is
    restored in \cref{cor:II.2-thm2-1-psi}. -/)
  (proof := /-- The assertion follows \cref{thm:II.2-2} because every module is flat over a
    field: $O/\bar Q=R/Q$ is a field, so the flatness conditions in (iii) and (iv) of
    \cref{thm:II.2-2} hold automatically. -/)
  (latexEnv := "corollary")]
theorem isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae_of_eq_maximalIdeal (hJP : J ≤ P) (hQ : Q = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) :
    [IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal P̄,
      HasInitialFormSystem J P Q,
      ∀ m : ℕ, Function.Bijective (grModMap Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) m)].TFAE := by
  have hPQ : P ≤ Q := hQ ▸ IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal_of_isPrime P
  have hQmax : Q.IsMaximal := hQ ▸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal.isMaximal R
  have hQbar : (Q̄).IsMaximal := isMaximal_map_quotientMk_of_le (hJP.trans hPQ)
  have hQbar' : (Q̄').IsMaximal := isMaximal_map_quotientMk_of_le (Ideal.map_mono hPQ)
  -- `O/Q̄` and `Ō/Q̄'` are fields, and every module over a field is free, hence flat.
  have hfree : IsNormallyFlatAlongIdeal Q̄ := fun p => by
    let _ : Field (𝒪 ⧸ Q̄) := Ideal.Quotient.field _
    exact Module.Free.of_divisionRing _ _
  have hflat : Module.Flat (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0) := by
    let _ : Field (Ō ⧸ Q̄') := Ideal.Quotient.field _
    have : Module.Free (Ō ⧸ Q̄') (GrModPiece Q̄' (AssocGraded P̄) 0) :=
      Module.Free.of_divisionRing _ _
    exact Module.Flat.of_free
  have h := isNormallyFlatAlongIdeal_tfae J P Q hJP hPQ
  have h12 := h.out 1 2
  have h23 := h.out 2 3
  have h31 := h.out 3 1
  tfae_have 1 → 2 := fun h₁ => (h12.mp h₁).2
  tfae_have 2 → 3 := fun h₂ => (h23.mp ⟨hfree, h₂⟩).2
  tfae_have 3 → 1 := fun h₃ => h31.mp ⟨hflat, h₃⟩
  tfae_finish

/-- Theorem 2 of p. 195 with its condition (ii), i.e. including Proposition 1 of §1 and Lemma 8:
